<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilton of Cadboll Stone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hilton of Cadboll Stone is a Pictish cross-slab of about 800 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-ross">Easter Ross</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Its surviving face shows a hunting scene with a woman riding side-saddle, trumpeters and animals, framed by vine-scroll and Pictish symbols; the cross on the other side was cut away in 1676 for a memorial. The upper part is in the National Museum of Scotland, and the lower part was excavated at the original site in 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilton-of-cadboll-stone.jpg" alt="Hilton of Cadboll Stone" width="320" height="598" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Catfish Jim and the soapdish · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilton_of_Cadboll_nms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The slab was raised on the east coast of the Tarbat Peninsula, in a natural amphitheatre about 100 m from the shore. The whole peninsula, from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cromarty-firth">Cromarty Firth</a> to Tarbatness, seems then to have been the estate of the monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portmahomack">Portmahomack</a>, and the stone was probably carved at its instigation. The Hilton of Cadboll Chapel was built 6 m to its east in the thirteenth century. Chips buried at the site show that defacing of the cross began in the late sixteenth century, during the Scottish Reformation, and a storm later felled the stone, breaking off its upper three-quarters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The surviving foot of the front shows the stepped base of a cross with bosses, from which a Calvary cross is inferred, surrounded, to judge from reconstructed fragments, by figures of Death, Judgement, Heaven and Hell. On the back a vine-scroll border, taken to stand for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, frames three panels beneath a double disc and Z-rod. The middle panel's rider seen face-on, long read as a female aristocrat because of the mirror symbol beside her, is identified by modern scholars with Christ or the Virgin Mary in a scene derived from the Roman Adventus; the deer harried by hounds recalls Psalm 42.</p><p>The bottom panel's scrollwork of triskeles, now the logo of the Glenmorangie distillery nearby, was found to spring from a cross at its centre and is read as the four rivers of paradise. Martin Goldberg has suggested that the slab served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> vigil, when converts were baptised and Psalm 42 was sung, since it stood near a stream where immersion was possible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Charles Cordiner brought the stone to public notice in his Antiquities and Scenery of the North of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> of 1780. In the late 1860s the Macleods of Cadboll moved the upper part to the garden of Invergordon Castle, and when the estate was sold in 1921 it was given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, until Scottish antiquaries had the gift redirected to the National Museum of Antiquities of Scotland.</p><p>Excavations by Kirkdale Archaeology and Glasgow University Archaeological Research Division between 1998 and 2001 recovered thousands of carved fragments as well as the lower portion, which allowed Isabel Henderson to infer the lost cross-face. The base is displayed locally rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, and a full-size copy by the sculptor Barry Grove stands near the original site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hilton of Cadboll Stone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pictish art is among the finest of early medieval Europe: the symbol stones and cross-slabs at Aberlemno, Meigle, <b>Hilton of Cadboll</b> and Nigg, with their mix of abstract symbols, interlace and lively animals, are unique to the region and form one of Scotland’s most recognisable heritage traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
